            <ArticleTitle>Abdominal pain in a patient with ankylosing spondylitis under treatment with infliximab.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>244-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/RHU.0b013e3181b12341</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In patients suffering from ankylosing spondylitis, silent inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is frequent. Furthermore, spondylarthritis may be the first manifestation of IBD.We describe the case of a patient suffering from ankylosing spondylitis who presented with abdominal pain. The patient had been treated over 2(1/2) years with infliximab. Although the initial clinical presentation seemed to suggest new-onset IBD as the cause of the abdominal pain, it eventuated that the patient was suffering from a severe abdominal manifestation of tuberculosis likely due to reactivation of latent tuberculosis by the anti-TNF agent.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
